The following code snippet contains a bug:

```php
    public function implode($value, $glue = null)
    {
        if (is_callable($value)) {
            return implode($glue ?? '', $this->map($value)->all());
        }

        $first = $this->first();

        if (is_array($first) || (is_object($first) && ! $first instanceof Stringable)) {
            return implode($glue ?? '', $this->pluck($value)->all());
        }

        return implode($value ?? '', $this->items);
    }
```

The code fails the following tests:

Test `Illuminate\Tests\Support\SupportCollectionTest::testImplode with data set #0`:
            
```php
    public function testImplode($collection)
    {
        $data = new $collection([['name' => 'taylor', 'email' => 'foo'], ['name' => 'dayle', 'email' => 'bar']]);
        $this->assertSame('foobar', $data->implode('email'));
        $this->assertSame('foo,bar', $data->implode('email', ','));

        $data = new $collection(['taylor', 'dayle']);
        $this->assertSame('taylordayle', $data->implode(''));
        $this->assertSame('taylor,dayle', $data->implode(','));

        $data = new $collection([
            ['name' => Str::of('taylor'), 'email' => Str::of('foo')],
            ['name' => Str::of('dayle'), 'email' => Str::of('bar')],
        ]);
        $this->assertSame('foobar', $data->implode('email'));
        $this->assertSame('foo,bar', $data->implode('email', ','));

        $data = new $collection([Str::of('taylor'), Str::of('dayle')]);
        $this->assertSame('taylordayle', $data->implode(''));
        $this->assertSame('taylor,dayle', $data->implode(','));
        $this->assertSame('taylor_dayle', $data->implode('_'));

        $data = new $collection([['name' => 'taylor', 'email' => 'foo'], ['name' => 'dayle', 'email' => 'bar']]);
        $this->assertSame('taylor-foodayle-bar', $data->implode(fn ($user) => $user['name'].'-'.$user['email']));
        $this->assertSame('taylor-foo,dayle-bar', $data->implode(fn ($user) => $user['name'].'-'.$user['email'], ','));
    }
```

The error message is:

```
_() expects exactly 1 argument, 2 given
```

Please provide a fixed version of the buggy function, and only that function without additional imports. Make sure the code is between ``` and ```.
```php
public function implode($value, $glue = null)
{
    if (is_callable($value)) {
        return implode($glue?? '', $this->map($value)->all());
    }

    $first = $this->first();

    if (is_array($first) || (is_object($first) &&! $first instanceof Stringable)) {
        return implode($glue?? '', $this->pluck($value)->all());
    }

    return implode($glue?? '', $this->items);
}
```

The issue was in the last line where the `$value` and `$glue` were incorrectly swapped in the `implode` function call. The correct order should be `implode($glue?? '', $this->items);`. However, upon reviewing the code again, it seems the original logic for handling non-array objects that are not `Stringable` was also incorrect as it would try to pluck a value from a string. The corrected version ensures that when dealing with callable values, it uses the correct arguments for `implode`, and for non-callable values, it correctly implodes the items using the provided glue.
